Build sccache on x32 (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnux32) and use the system
OpenSSL/LibreSSL for all cryptography.

* TLS: reqwest, opendal's reqwest transport and redis use native-tls
  (openssl crate) instead of rustls + aws-lc-rs. aws-lc-sys's C code assumes
  64-bit pointers on x86_64 and fails to compile for x32.
* JWT (sccache-dist): jsonwebtoken 9 hard-depends on ring, which has no x32
  support. Move to jsonwebtoken 11 without a built-in backend and install an
  OpenSSL-backed CryptoProvider (src/dist/jwt_openssl.rs): HS256/384/512,
  RS256/384/512, PS256/384/512. Validation.insecure_disable_signature_validation
  is gone in 11; use jwt::dangerous::insecure_decode instead.
* redis: opendal only re-exports services::Redis for its rustls feature, so
  depend on opendal-service-redis directly with native-tls.
* blake3: its x86_64 assembly reads 8-byte pointers from the input pointer
  array and segfaults (or misbehaves) on x32; enable prefer_intrinsics there.
* thirtyfour (dev-dependency): use native-tls instead of rustls/ring.
* Cargo.lock: jsonwebtoken 9.2.0 -> 11.1.0, adds zeroize_derive 1.5.0, drops
  aws-lc-rs, aws-lc-sys, cmake, quinn*, rustls-platform-verifier, webpki-roots.

